WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:06.580
MELANIE PARKER: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.189
My name is Melanie Parker.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.710
I'm Chief Diversity
Officer for Google.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:14.130
I'm joined by James Heighington,
our Global Head of Diversity

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.900
Strategy for this session.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.090
All of you in this
session today know

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.730
the demand for cloud
technology solutions

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:25.100
has ramped up dramatically
these few months.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.260
The need for reliable tools that
help teams collaborate remotely

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.300
has never been greater.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.550
As your companies make
decisions about whether you're

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.220
working from home
or the office, you

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.970
might be starting to wonder how
that will impact your workplace

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.520
culture.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.130
You're probably
asking questions like,

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:47.730
how's the real value of my
businesses workplace culture?

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.250
How might we continue
to foster community

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.550
and belonging with a more
distributed workforce?

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.540
How are my company's decisions
influencing its commitments

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.990
related to diversity,
equity, and inclusion?

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.350
I'll be upfront.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:03.910
I don't have all the answers.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:07.470
I'm wrestling with the
same questions, too.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.810
In fact, our CEO, Sundar Pichai,
was recently asked about this.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.970
He mentioned how
our campuses have

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.750
been designed to foster
collaboration and community, as

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.030
well as chance encounters
that lead to innovation.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:23.610
But he also said
that we're looking

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.120
at the data and the feedback
from employees with an eye

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.790
toward offering more
flexibility to work

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.200
from home in the future.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.400
These conversations are ongoing.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.340
And there are still many
unknowns about the future.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.150
But today, what is
clear is we need

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.060
to have a deep understanding
of our workforce

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:47.020
before we begin to insert
DEI into the dialogue

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:49.560
in a meaningful way.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.460
And one of the key ways we
reveal our understanding

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.380
of our workforce
and the progress

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.740
we're making through DEI is
through our Diversity Annual

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:58.880
Report.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.260
We've published it
the past six years.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.120
And we just recently
released the 2020 report.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.370
So today, I want
to share with you

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.789
how we're building a workforce
that reflects all communities,

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:12.860
as well as some of
the DEI strategies

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:17.810
we're using to cultivate a
sense of belonging at this time.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:22.100
Every year since 2014, we've
published our Diversity Annual

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.150
Report.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.900
We were one of the first
to do so in our industry.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.970
The report is
anchored on the fact

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:31.430
that there's some
underrepresented groups who

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.320
have historically
been discriminated

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.350
against and excluded
from opportunities

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:41.870
and who today remain affected
by systemic racism, xenophobia,

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:43.460
and the like.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.220
We continually try
to look at our data

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:49.490
and capture more nuances
about all of the people who

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:50.600
work at Google.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.960
We always want to see more
humanity in the report.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:58.340
Everyone wants to see themselves
reflected and validated.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.460
I should also note that we
have supplements to this data,

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.510
including a survey called
Self-ID, which is global.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.400
Through it, we're
starting to expand

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.200
how we look at our workforce.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.840
So with that backdrop, I
want to share with you what

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.740
we're learning along the way.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:18.760
This report provides one of the
largest publicly available DEI

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.370
data sets in our industry.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.230
This data represents real people
who make real contributions

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.450
to our mission and our culture.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.760
And we must keep in
mind that human element.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.550
Data drives everything
Google does, from the way

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:36.560
we develop products
to the way we

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.310
design our diversity, equity,
and inclusion efforts.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:44.270
Before we dive in, a couple
quick notes on our data--

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:48.860
first, we include gender data
globally and race in the US.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:52.010
Around the world, there are
various government protections

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.910
on what data we must collect,
as well as what data we cannot

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:56.900
collect.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.230
This report is aligned with
standard business practices

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.990
for multinational
corporations, even

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:06.350
as we find new ways to collect
more globally inclusive

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.180
data through Self-ID.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.780
Second, we use a plus sign
in all our racial categories

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.569
to indicate multiracial
people are plussed

00:04:16.569 --> 00:04:19.510
in to each racial category.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.000
Previously,
multiracial people were

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.000
counted in a single
category, which doesn't fully

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.190
represent their identity.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.460
We made this change
to be more inclusive.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:34.000
With DEI, most people think
of the data surrounding

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.370
how our workforce is
growing to better reflect

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:38.110
the communities we serve.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:40.900
These changes in our
workforce representation

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:46.000
are largely driven by two
inputs, hiring and attrition.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.770
Let's start with hiring.

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:51.860
Google did well in
several key ways in 2019

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.600
when it comes to hiring.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.930
We have both tech and
nontech jobs at Google.

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.990
Last year, we saw the largest
increase for Black tech

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.510
hiring we've ever measured.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:06.710
We also look at new hires, our
"newglers," as we call them.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.570
We're pleased that
Black newglers now

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.570
make up the largest
underrepresented racial group

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:13.580
in nontech hiring.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.930
We measure success
in other ways, too.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.900
For example, a
Googler-built tool

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.630
helped us identify
and remove gender bias

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:23.270
from job postings, which
helped us to increase

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.520
women applicants for many jobs.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.790
And our internship program,
which is remote this summer,

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:31.880
continued to build
broader pathways to tech.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.520
We still want to see
stronger hiring growth

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:37.040
in some underrepresented
groups, such as

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:38.780
Native American Googlers.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.540
And we'll try to focus
on that this year.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:44.300
In addition to hiring,
it's equally important

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:47.840
to focus on retaining Googlers
from all underrepresented

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.460
groups.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:52.490
With that in mind,
our Latinx community

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.800
is the first
underrepresented group

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.930
to see attrition
rates for both women

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:01.130
and men below the average,
a positive development,

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:05.330
even though there was a slight
increase for Latinx women.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:09.320
Women on average at Google
still had lower attrition rates

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:10.610
than men.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:13.850
At the same time, attrition
rates for Black women

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:17.350
are now higher than
average attrition rates.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:20.950
How do we do better with
regards to retention?

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:24.470
One strategy is we
build on what works.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:28.090
For example, we have what we
call "retention equity teams."

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:31.810
84% of Googlers who went
through that program

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:33.940
have decided to stay at Google.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:36.470
We're already building
on that number this year,

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:38.980
which is critically
important because retention

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:41.500
is going to play a
key role in helping us

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.930
achieve our aspirational goals.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:46.890
We've covered hiring
and attrition.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:50.640
Let's see where Google landed
with representation overall.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.310
We saw continued growth in
representation for women

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:57.780
globally, as well as for Black
and Latinx Googlers in the US.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:01.410
We also had the largest increase
in Black+ representation

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:06.810
overall and in technical roles
since we began publishing.

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:10.140
For the second year in a row,
we maintained our growth rate

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:13.540
of representation of women
in leadership roles globally.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:17.520
We also see further proof that
our internal mobility programs

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.810
are paying off.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.750
Last year, the increase
in women leadership

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:25.740
was attributable mostly
to promotions, not hiring.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.290
And this year, we see a similar
trend for our Latinx leaders.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:33.680
Tracking the annual data
is very useful in working

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:36.470
towards goals in
hiring, retention,

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.690
and overall representation.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:43.270
Let's zoom out of it and look
at trends over multiple years.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.600
The fact is, in recent
years, we have scaled up

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.270
our diversity, equity,
and inclusion initiatives

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:53.110
to match the pace
of Google's growth.

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:56.470
Since 2014, Google's
global headcount

00:07:56.470 --> 00:08:04.540
has increased by 170% and
our US headcount by 180%.

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:08.500
As you can see, the growth rate
of underrepresented Googlers

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.150
has generally grown even faster.

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:13.510
This demonstrates an
ongoing commitment

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:17.560
to what we call "responsible
growth," with these percentage

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:21.520
points representing thousands
of new underrepresented Googlers

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:22.870
joining.

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:25.340
This data is
critically important.

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:28.330
And I hope all of you
have taken that first step

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:31.870
to start gathering data to
help guide your DEI strategies

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:33.380
and goals.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:36.250
But obviously, the work
doesn't stop there.

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:38.500
In Google's case,
we're actually working

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:42.039
in a larger context of our
societal responsibility

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:45.970
to build a robust, diverse
talent pool for our industry.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:49.000
We must not only focus on
Google's immediate hiring

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:53.740
needs, but also on long-term,
systemic solutions.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:55.330
Consider this.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:59.650
Someone born in 1998, the
year Google was founded,

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:02.230
is only now
completing university.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:04.690
Preparing the
next-generation workforce

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:07.660
for the increasingly
technical nature of work

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:10.630
requires long-term
partnerships with government,

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.420
educational initiatives,
policymakers,

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:15.280
and community organizations.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.980
And on that note, I want to turn
it over to James Heighington.

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:20.300
JAMES HEIGHINGTON: Thank you.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.640
As Melanie stated,
we view our work

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.030
within the larger context of
our societal responsibility

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:29.330
that we have at Google to
build a robust, diverse talent

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.560
pool for our industry.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.480
And that's why, in 2019, we
strengthened our partnerships

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:39.020
with both local organizations
and local communities.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.300
And we started to expand
the vision of our work

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.170
to include not only education,
but entire ecosystems

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.960
in the communities
that we call home.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.820
We're addressing these
systemic challenges head-on.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.600
And we're doing so in order
to responsibly grow our talent

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.460
and meet industry demand.

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:57.680
This past year, we
implemented versions

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:01.550
of this holistic strategy in
places like Atlanta, London,

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:03.830
and cities around
India by engaging

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:06.050
with not just the
educational sector,

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:09.020
but with local businesses,
community organizations,

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:12.410
elected officials, even urban
planning and real estate

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:13.580
developers.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.980
This reflects how Google
and these communities

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:18.860
can come together to
invest in shared success

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:21.350
and realize mutual benefits.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:23.930
The issues that we're
facing are systemic.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.990
And it's our responsibility to
help address them and invest

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.420
back into the communities
and infrastructure that

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.160
support our growth.

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:34.340
As Google continues to grow,
we have a responsibility

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:37.820
to scale our diversity, equity,
and inclusion initiatives

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:40.250
and increase pathways to
tech in the communities

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:42.260
that Google calls home.

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:44.840
It's important to think
about the end state here

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:47.270
of what we're actually working
towards when we think about

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:49.590
diversity, equity,
and inclusion.

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.370
This is not about diversity
just for the sake of diversity.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:55.790
This is not just about the heads
that we have sitting at desks.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:58.460
But this is about creating
a real sense of value

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:01.040
for the experiences
and expertise

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:03.830
that everybody brings
to their work every day.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:05.930
And those experiences
and expertise

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:09.050
can help improve our company,
our products, and the services

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:10.550
that we provide.

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:12.920
But in order to truly
unlock that value

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:14.990
from every one of
our employees, we

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:17.180
need to ensure that
everyone feels included.

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:18.890
We need to ensure
that everyone feels

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:21.410
like they belong at Google.

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:23.230
Inclusion means everyone.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:25.040
And that also means
the job of creating

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:27.870
an inclusive environment
is a job for everyone.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:29.720
At Google, we believe
this is not just

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.460
the role of the diversity team.

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:34.220
This is a shared responsibility
among every employee

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:35.762
that works at our company.

00:11:35.762 --> 00:11:37.220
And what we need
to do is make sure

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:39.620
that we are not just
accelerating this work,

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:41.720
but that we are
expanding this work

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:43.790
and doing so so
that every Googler--

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:46.610
in particular, those from
underrepresented groups--

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:49.520
experiences an
inclusive workplace.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:52.490
On this journey, we can
measure how we are doing.

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:54.500
And we do measure
how we're doing

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:56.660
through a series
of internal indexes

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:59.630
that we have created here
at Google and also by using

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:01.490
external indexes that we have.

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:05.660
For example, in 2019, we were
proud to file the Disability

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:07.250
Equality Index.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:10.500
And we use these indexes
to benchmark our efforts

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.740
and make improvements
as we go along.

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:14.410
These indexes
aren't an end state.

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:18.140
They are guideposts that
we use on our journey.

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:21.290
When we started reporting
this data in 2014,

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:24.470
we used the data that we had
available on hand at that time.

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:26.420
And as Melanie mentioned,
that's the data

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:29.030
that we had collected through
mandatory government reporting

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:30.440
obligations.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:32.910
But as our understanding
of diversity, equity,

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:34.970
and inclusion has
grown, so, too,

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:38.430
has the need for a more
robust and inclusive data set.

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:41.160
And we've responded by
introducing a new data set.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.040
And that is Self-ID.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:46.520
The goal of this is to have
a more global and more fully

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:50.390
inclusive data set that we
can use to expand our DEI

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:52.490
work in a data-driven way.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:55.070
Google is one of the only
companies in our industry

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:58.547
to collect this amount of
information on a global scale.

00:12:58.547 --> 00:13:01.130
And here's how we've been able
to put some of this data to you

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:02.300
so far.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:04.640
We've hosted five
Women of Color Summits

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:07.100
around the globe
over the past year

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:09.770
to ensure that we are
focusing on the intersectional

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:12.680
experiences of women of
color at all the Google

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:14.510
offices that we have.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:16.730
We've also used this
data to continue

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:18.860
to expand the
company-wide goals that we

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:20.660
have around accessibility.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:24.200
In addition, we use this data
to analyze the company pulse

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:27.290
survey that we do every year
and ensure that we are looking

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:30.290
at results by race and gender,
in addition to things like

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:33.350
disability status,
nonbinary genders,

00:13:33.350 --> 00:13:37.100
veterans and military service,
for the LGBTQ+ communities,

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:39.122
and age as well.

00:13:39.122 --> 00:13:41.240
When we also think
of inclusive, it's

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:42.950
important that we
are being inclusive

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:45.080
of more demographic identities.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:46.550
But we also want
to ensure that we

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:48.200
are being inclusive
in more regions

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:51.440
and taking a global
approach of our DEI work.

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:54.080
At Google, we have more
than 100,000 employees

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:56.390
spanning nearly 60 countries.

00:13:56.390 --> 00:14:00.350
Operating at this level brings
an extra layer of complexity

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:02.270
to everything that we do.

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:04.490
At the same time, we
need to balance that

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:06.980
by recognizing diversity,
equity, and inclusion

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:09.170
is unique to every region.

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:11.360
We need to ensure that
we are understanding

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:13.550
each local context
and developing

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:15.920
local strategies in response.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:18.610
And this year's diversity
report reflects that work,

00:14:18.610 --> 00:14:21.220
with nearly half of the
content coming from outside

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:22.990
of the United States.

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:25.390
What you see on the screen
is just part of the work

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:27.400
that we've done
over this past year.

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:29.930
We are continuing to
invest in this work.

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:32.620
And we are committed to
telling more global DEI

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:35.750
stories about our
work as we go forward.

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:37.840
All of the work that
we've been talking about

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:40.420
highlights how we are building
a culture of belonging.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:42.760
And for more on that, I'll
turn it over to Melanie

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:44.540
to wrap us up.

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:46.140
MELANIE PARKER: Thank you, Jim.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:48.590
As we move further
into 2020, Google

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:50.660
is anchoring on the
common thread that

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:53.150
binds all of our DEI efforts.

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:55.730
Belonging describes a
vision that we continually

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:57.230
aim to achieve.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:15:00.440
We can't see our colleagues
in person due to COVID-19.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:02.480
But it's inspiring
to me to see how

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.510
our Googlers are finding
creative ways to cultivate

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:07.020
a sense of belonging.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:09.380
For example, our
employee resource groups

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:12.590
are hosting music,
trivia, and dance parties

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:15.770
on Google Meet, our
video conferencing tool.

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:17.960
Groups of Googlers are
also pulling together

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:21.500
to volunteer their time and
expertise to important causes

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:24.770
that help communities
affected by COVID-19.

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:28.430
And on a larger scale,
Google and google.org

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:30.980
are supporting many
organizations and communities

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:33.680
within Google and
across the globe,

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:36.260
leveraging our
tech and our funds.

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:39.200
I'm sure that many of your
companies and your employees

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.270
have been engaged in
similar activities

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:43.580
to the ones I just described.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:46.280
The COVID-19 situation
will continue

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:48.980
to shape all of our DEI work.

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:51.590
For Google, that means
we must continually

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:54.500
renew our commitment
to serving communities

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:58.460
with the greatest needs and
ensuring equity for all.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:02.480
We have to take steps to help
Googlers feel like they belong,

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:04.940
even if they aren't physically
back in a Google office

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:06.080
just yet.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:09.170
We want to understand
what belonging truly means

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:12.530
and what needs to be done so
that all Googlers can continue

00:16:12.530 --> 00:16:16.913
to help us cocreate our culture
and that we leave no one out.

00:16:16.913 --> 00:16:18.830
I hope that some of the
information that we've

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:22.130
shared in our session today
has spurred some ideas for you

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:24.890
to consider in
your own DEI work,

00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:27.410
from how you track
workforce representation

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:29.750
to your global DEI strategies.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:31.870
Have a great day.

